<article class="node node--type-landing-page node--view-mode-full">
  
      




<div  class="hero hero--standard hero--blue">
  <div class="hero__text-container">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__text-wrap">
        
                  <h1 class="hero__title">Transforming how we share Ontario’s securities regulation</h1>
        
        
        
              </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>


      




<section id="section543626"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543626">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <p class="text-align-right"><a href="/sites/default/files/2026-03/transforming-how-we-share-Ontario-securities-regulation_20260330_0.pdf">Download PDF</a></p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



      


<div class="tab-container tab-container--default">
    <div class="content">
      <ul class="nav nav-tabs print-hidden" role="tablist">
              <li role="tab"><a id="nav-tab-543391" data-toggle="tab" href="#tabContent543391" aria-controls="#tabContent543391">
      Our approach
  </a></li>

              <li role="tab"><a id="nav-tab-543416" data-toggle="tab" href="#tabContent543416" aria-controls="#tabContent543416">
      Use cases
  </a></li>

              <li role="tab"><a id="nav-tab-543476" data-toggle="tab" href="#tabContent543476" aria-controls="#tabContent543476">
      Business requirements  
  </a></li>

              <li role="tab"><a id="nav-tab-543486" data-toggle="tab" href="#tabContent543486" aria-controls="#tabContent543486">
      Example framework  
  </a></li>

              <li role="tab"><a id="nav-tab-543576" data-toggle="tab" href="#tabContent543576" aria-controls="#tabContent543576">
      Provide feedback
  </a></li>

        </ul>
  <div class="tab-content">
    
      

<div class="tab-item tab-item--default tab-pane fade" id="tabContent543391" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="nav-tab-543391">
      <h2 class="print-only">
      Our approach
  </h2>
        <div class="tab-item__content">
      
      




<section id="section543386"  class="text-section text-section--default with-bullet container section section--default has-anchor section543386">
    
  
    <div class="title">
    <h2>Our approach</h2>
  </div>
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <p>The OSC is exploring ways to facilitate access to our regulatory framework and reduce burden for capital markets participants by publishing a machine-readable dataset of our regulatory instruments, as noted in the <a href="https://www.osc.ca/en/securities-law/instruments-rules-policies/1/11-7101/osc-notice-11-7101-statement-priorities-request-comments-regarding-statement-priorities-fiscal-year" target="_blank">Request for Comments on our Statement of Priorities for 2026-2027</a>. This effort is intended to reduce compliance costs for market participants and mitigate barriers to entry for service providers, while supporting the development of new and existing Regulatory Technology (RegTech) solutions for investors to make more informed decisions about investment products and services.</p><p>We understand that many market participants have proprietary databases containing relevant regulatory documents and use automation to maintain compliance with regulatory requirements. A machine-readable dataset can support market participants in these endeavors by simplifying the ingestion of OSC regulatory instruments and assisting users to interpret those instruments. It also allows users to understand connections between regulatory documents—such as by connecting statutes to regulations or connecting national instruments to staff notices.</p><h3>Why build a machine-readable dataset?</h3><p>Building a machine-readable dataset means translating our regulatory instruments from the existing PDF and HTML files into a structured format that allows systems to process the relevant sections of statutes, regulations, rules, and policies.</p><p>Encoded in formats such as JSON or XML, the dataset will include not just the words of every rule and regulation, but also the connections between them.</p><p>Structure makes data easier to reuse, search, and analyze. Each part is usually tagged with metadata that includes elements such as labels, linkages and other elements that provide context on meaning and relations. A familiar example of metadata is the location and time information saved with each photo on your phone. This is structured data, when information is broken down into smaller, clearly defined parts and organized in a consistent, predefined way.</p><p>We are in the early stages of piloting categorizing and labeling words or phrases in our regulatory framework so that a computer can recognize its syntactic, semantic, and/or pragmatic significance (see table).</p><h3>Human-Readable Text vs. Plain Text vs. Metadata-Tagged Text</h3><table class="table table-hover" style="width:100%px;" border="1" cellspacing="4" cellpadding="4"><thead><tr style="background-color:#c6d9f1;"><th style="width:30%;"><strong>Exclusively Human-Readable&nbsp;</strong></th><th style="width:30%;"><strong>Plain Text</strong></th><th style="width:40%;"><strong>Metadata-Tagged Text</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><img style="border:1px solid #000000;" src="/sites/default/files/inline-images/The%20quick%20brown%20fox.png" data-entity-uuid="4285b545-5a2e-4bf8-b88f-56d377397e6d" data-entity-type="file" alt="The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" width="225" height="33" border="1" loading="lazy"><p><em>This image can be read by most humans (taking into account visual disability, language differences, etc.) but is unintelligible to a computer without extra analysis.</em></p></td><td style="width:158px;">The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.</td><td style="width:227px;">&lt;subject&gt;The quick brown fox&lt;/subject&gt; &lt;verb&gt;jumps&lt;/verb&gt; over &lt;object&gt;the lazy dog.&lt;/object&gt;</td></tr></tbody></table><p><br>&nbsp;</p><p>The categories applied to one section of an instrument may differ from those applied to another, depending on its intended purpose. These categories, collectively known as the ontology, contain the vocabulary needed to describe a given document’s contents. An instrument with a proper format based on a shared taxonomy with others can facilitate faster information retrieval and lead to more efficient processing and analysis of information.</p><h3>Supporting compliance and enhancing competitiveness</h3><p>A machine-readable dataset of regulatory instruments has the potential to enhance stakeholder access, searchability, and understanding of the connections between regulatory instruments, making it easier to comply with regulatory requirements. The accelerating adoption and availability of Artificial Intelligence systems and tools has increased the need for authoritative, reliable, structured regulatory data that is interpretable by computers—including machine learning algorithms.</p><p>The OSC strives to support an environment where access to a machine-readable dataset supports a broad range of use cases, helping to close the gap between industry and the regulatory ecosystem. That could include capital market participants developing business models and tools that increase competition and efficiency in the market, or service providers developing new and existing tools that enhance the investor experience.</p><p>The benefits of enhanced access to the regulatory framework have the potential to increase transparency and availability of data for research and analysis—even in areas we haven’t thought of yet.</p><div style="border:2px solid #002d42;margin-bottom:15px;padding:20px;"><p>We want to hear your feedback on machine-readable rules, including from stakeholders who will directly benefit from this new publication format.</p><p>For further information, please see the accompanying tabs for lessons from our existing engagements with both external and internal stakeholders on this project and the process of making our current regulatory framework machine readable.</p><p>While our feedback period has ended, we continue to accept comments on an ongoing basis at <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#1d6f787a68717c69726f64696f7c736e7b726f707c697472735d726e7e337e7c"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="90e2f5f7e5fcf1e4ffe2e9e4e2f1fee3f6ffe2fdf1e4f9fffed0ffe3f3bef3f1">[email&#160;protected]</span></a>.</p><p>Your feedback will play a critical role in shaping a future-ready regulatory system.</p><p>Please refer your questions to:</p><p>Levin Karg&nbsp;<br>Head of Modernizing Regulation&nbsp;<br>Regulatory Transformation and Strategic Research<br><a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#dab6b1bba8bd9ab5a9b9f4bdb5acf4b5b4f4b9bb"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="e4888f859683a48b9787ca8785">[email&#160;protected]</span></a></p><p>Christina Furlano<br>Senior Knowledge Analyst<br>Regulatory Transformation and Strategic Research&nbsp;<br><a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#7b181d0e09171a15143b14081855181a"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="4f2c293a3d232e21200f203c2c612c2e">[email&#160;protected]</span></a>&nbsp;</p></div>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>


      

<div class="tab-item tab-item--default tab-pane fade" id="tabContent543416" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="nav-tab-543416">
      <h2 class="print-only">
      Use cases
  </h2>
        <div class="tab-item__content">
      
      




<section id="section543411"  class="text-section text-section--default with-bullet container section section--default has-anchor section543411">
    
  
    <div class="title">
    <h2>Use cases</h2>
  </div>
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <blockquote style="border:2px solid #002d42;margin-bottom:15px;padding:20px;"><p>The OSC is exploring the idea of publishing a machine-readable dataset of our regulatory instruments to facilitate access to our regulatory framework and reduce burden for capital markets participants. We are seeking input from stakeholders on our initial explorations and next steps. We encourage you to review the sample metadata schema, provide feedback on licensing preferences and use cases, identify priority content for conversion to a machine-readable format, and participate in future consultations on taxonomy governance and implementation strategy.</p></blockquote><h3>How a machine-readable regulatory framework could be used</h3><p>This article details our work on understanding who would use a machine-readable dataset and how they would use it.</p><p>Different use cases for this dataset require distinct data attributes and functionalities. Both the design and the distribution of the data should account for two tiers of use cases:</p><p><strong>First-tier use cases</strong> (see Table 1) relate to commercial users seeking to incorporate regulatory content into their compliance solutions or analytical tools. The key user personas involved are universally software engineers and data scientists, with occasional support from regulatory subject matter experts.</p><p><strong>Second-tier use cases </strong>(see Table 2) relate to users using technology to support regulatory intake and research, compliance workflows, policy and risk management tasks as well as oversight and internal audit tasks. Table 2 summarises the full set of second tier use cases identified in research workshops, and the sense of relative priority among those. The workshops organised as part of this work sought to associate use cases with specific data attributes, resulting in the more detailed requirements that follow<strong>.</strong></p><h3>Table 1: First tier use cases</h3><table class="table table-hover" style="width:100%;" border="1" cellspacing="4" cellpadding="4"><thead><tr style="background-color:#c6d9f1;"><th style="width:25%;"><strong>High level category</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>First-tier use case</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>Primary user personas</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>Purpose/function</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application development</td><td>Ontology import</td><td>Data Scientist / Software Engineer</td><td>Ingest OSC taxonomies, abstracted from the regulatory framework, for use in search and navigation interfaces for end users, e.g. dropdown menus.</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Application development</td><td>Prototyping</td><td>Data Scientist / Software Engineer</td><td>Rapidly visualise the result of small scale, ad-hoc document queries in order to understand search behaviour and build a prototype user interface. Integrate rules and regulations into a prototype of a new products regulatory sandbox, so that financial institutions can test their product against current rules and regulations with instant feedback&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Application development</td><td>API admin facility</td><td>Data Scientist / Software Engineer / OSC Digital Rulebook team</td><td>Give developers an API endpoint through which user requests can be permissioned, monitored, logged and reported on, in order to manage users, pricing, terms of use.</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Application development</td><td>Language model training and fine-tuning</td><td>Data Scientist / Software Engineer</td><td>Allow developers / data scientists to extract in a single, bulk request, a corpus of structured content, filtering out any unneeded or irrelevant documents at the origin, so that they can fine tune language models effectively while lowering processing costs and time.</td></tr><tr><td>Application development</td><td>Source of Truth&nbsp;</td><td>Data Scientist / Software Engineer</td><td>Allow developers to make a corpus of structured content available to a chatbot, complete with thematic tags, so that the chatbot can easily identify documents that are good candidates for information retrieval and only process those in its responses.</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Data visualisation&nbsp;</td><td>Document graph</td><td>OSC Digital Rulebook team / Digital Publishing team</td><td>Use document metadata to cluster similar regulatory content and draw connections via citations, in order to provide a graph of dependencies, if any.</td></tr><tr><td>Policy analysis and development</td><td>Impact assessment</td><td>Regulatory economist / Data Scientist&nbsp;</td><td>Integrate regulatory content into a prototype simulating how new rules could affect existing business lines. This allows users to use the data in a digital twin simulation for policy impact analysis. </td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Table 2: Second tier use cases</h3><table class="table table-hover" style="width:100%;" border="1" cellspacing="4" cellpadding="4"><thead><tr style="background-color:#c6d9f1;"><th style="width:25%;"><strong>High level category</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>First-tier use case</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>Primary user personas</strong></th><th style="width:25%;"><strong>Purpose/function</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regulatory content management and processing</td><td>Regulatory Horizon Scanning &amp; Intake</td><td>Compliance generalists</td><td>Identify and route relevant regulatory updates using metadata</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Regulatory content management and processing</td><td>Regulatory Policy Management</td><td>Policy owners / SMEs</td><td>Assess and revise internal policies based on new regulations</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory content management and processing</td><td>Current State Assessment &amp; Change Management&nbsp;</td><td>SMEs / Control owners</td><td>Review and update internal guidance and controls</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Task &amp; Work Management</td><td>Compliance Task Overview &amp; Monitoring</td><td>Compliance managers / oversight functions</td><td>Track task performance and audit decision-making</td></tr><tr><td>Task &amp; Work Management</td><td>Regulatory / Compliance Risk Dashboard</td><td>Risk managers / oversight functions</td><td>Monitor risk indicators and prioritize remediation</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Task &amp; Work Management</td><td>Control Framework Ingestion &amp; Maintenance</td><td>Business analysts / risk managers (e.g., cybersecurity, privacy)</td><td>Extract and integrate control frameworks into Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC) platforms</td></tr><tr><td>Lineage &amp; Impact Tracking</td><td>Policies/Controls Lineage &amp; Impact Assessment</td><td>Risk managers / oversight functions</td><td>Trace regulatory origins of obligations or controls and assess legal risk</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Lineage &amp; Impact Tracking</td><td>Customer Outcome Tracing</td><td>Risk managers / product owners / oversight functions</td><td>Link adverse outcomes to failed controls or unmet obligations</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Datapoint-Level Change Management</td><td>Information Asset Impact Assessment</td><td>Data owners</td><td>Identify changes to data collection and reporting due to new regulations</td></tr><tr><td>Datapoint-Level Change Management</td><td>Regulatory Content &amp; Reporting Framework Alignment</td><td>Reporting taxonomy owners / data engineers</td><td>Detect and manage changes to reporting frameworks</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Datapoint-Level Change Management</td><td>Automated Contract &amp; Clause Review Rules Engine Integration</td><td>Technical specialists (internal &amp; vendors)</td><td>Extract granular rule logic for implementation</td></tr><tr><td>Datapoint-Level Change Management</td><td>Business Requirements Assessment</td><td>Procurement teams / technologists / data scientists / asset owners</td><td>Consolidate data structure, governance, and reporting needs in order to understand what systems must be built or procured</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Regulatory Benchmarking &amp; Equivalence Assessment</td><td>Regulatory policy owners and technical specialists</td><td>Compare domestic regulation with international standards</td></tr><tr><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Regulatory Antecedents Research</td><td>Academics / legal / public affairs professionals</td><td>Study influences on regulatory text formation</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Regulatory Network Analysis</td><td>Academics / legal / data scientists</td><td>Map citations and cross-references between texts</td></tr><tr><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Rule Complexity &amp; Burden Assessment</td><td>Regulatory economists / public affairs teams</td><td>Evaluate regulatory burden and enforceability</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Regulatory Impact Assessments</td><td>Academics / regulatory economists</td><td>Assess market impact of regulatory interventions</td></tr><tr><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Document indexing</td><td>Academics / regulatory economists</td><td>Increase the re-usability of disparate regulatory texts by supporting granular level thematic and parameter searches.&nbsp;</td></tr><tr style="background-color:#f2f2f2;"><td>Research and Information Retrieval</td><td>Market or Product-Level Decision Support</td><td>Commercial analysts / product champions</td><td>Evaluate regulatory viability of new products or market expansions</td></tr></tbody></table>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>


      

<div class="tab-item tab-item--default tab-pane fade" id="tabContent543476" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="nav-tab-543476">
      <h2 class="print-only">
      Business requirements  
  </h2>
        <div class="tab-item__content">
      
      




<section id="section543426"  class="text-section text-section--default with-bullet container section section--default has-anchor section543426">
    
  
    <div class="title">
    <h2>Business requirements  </h2>
  </div>
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <blockquote style="border:2px solid #002d42;margin-bottom:15px;padding:20px;"><p>The OSC is exploring the idea of publishing a machine-readable dataset of our regulatory instruments to facilitate access to our regulatory framework and reduce burden for capital markets participants. We are seeking input from stakeholders on our initial explorations and next steps. We encourage you to review the sample metadata schema, provide feedback on licensing preferences and use cases, identify priority content for conversion to a machine-readable format, and participate in future consultations on taxonomy governance and implementation strategy.</p></blockquote><h3>What a machine-readable dataset needs to be useful</h3><p>This article details the business requirements we developed from existing feedback through many channels including a senior-level industry roundtable; technical workshops with vendors, industry and consultants; and targeted interviews with academics and others.</p><p>The strategic, operational, and logistical/tactical requirements for a machine-readable data set of our regulatory instruments are brought together below into a detailed statement of business requirements. These span all the program outputs, namely</p><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha;" type="a"><li data-list-item-id="ead273917f7796f6fcb9d35b7b798ec7f">A database of structured, machine-readable OSC regulatory content – combining both the primary text and any metadata overlayed over the text during the publishing process.</li><li data-list-item-id="efd88f4e444c89fd33f054ef572e1e477">An Application Programming Interface (API) providing access to the structured document database in &nbsp;in response to standard queries; or alternatives based on, e.g. cloud-based file drops</li><li data-list-item-id="e34afcbdd924addef5e5d5e6934a810af">Documentation for the structured content available alongside the database in a), allowing application and solution providers to anticipate the key data attributes and structures available in the text and how these might be surfaced in their applications.</li><li data-list-item-id="ea4b13a25fbec8cbb74934f21979dd364">Template license agreements, disclaimers and terms of use that set out the obligations of licensees and allow for different approaches and business models.</li></ol>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



      






<section id="section543471"  class="card-collection card-collection--default card-collection--accordion-card card-collection-layout-1-col transparent section section--default has-anchor section543471">
      <div class="container">

    
<div class="section-header">
    
    </div>


    <div class="card-collection__content_wrapper">
              <div class="accordion" id="accordionGroup543471">
          
      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543431" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543431" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543431">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Granular structure &amp; metadata 
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543431" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543431">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Clause-level granularity and semantic structure</h4><p>Structured documents should be divided into hierarchical sections, preserving the original author’s intended clause-level structure in document metadata.</p><h4>Data transcripts</h4><p>To enhance usability, structured documents should preserve the raw text of the original document (the transcript) for use by both human readers and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems.</p><h4>Authoritative metadata</h4><p>Structured documents should include metadata on the applicability of content to different entity types, versioning artifacts, and on the currency and legal validity of texts that only the regulator can provide authoritatively.</p><h4>Temporal metadata</h4><p>Structured documents should cite publication, consultation, and effective dates, including phased implementations, allowing users to reconstruct a timeline of regulatory change.</p><h4>OSC controlled vocabularies &amp; ontologies</h4><p>Structured documents should use authoritative controlled vocabularies to identify affected parties and activities (e.g., firm registration types).</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543436" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543436" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543436">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Cross-referencing &amp; lineage tracking 
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543436" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543436">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Document lineage &amp; validity</h4><p>Structured documents should allow users to trace the evolution of policies and rules, with metadata indicating the document type (such as e.g. consultations), and linkages between documents.</p><h4>Citation networks and mapping to upstream documents</h4><p>Structured documents should enable tracing of references across rules, guidance, and eventually consultations. They should also link rules to enabling legislation or higher-level legal frameworks in a manner that can be distinguished from simple citation.</p><h4>Potential for tracing consultation inputs</h4><p>In a future iteration, structured documents could assist in determining how stakeholder input and legal/policy context shape rules by relating regulatory texts to the consultation documents that preceded them and, through those, to the public stakeholder comments received during consultation.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543441" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543441" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543441">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Support for analysis of comparative regulation &amp; regulatory diffusion   
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543441" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543441">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Cross-jurisdiction references</h4><p>Subject to permissions, selected regulatory content outside the OSC’s jurisdiction can be onboarded where needed to correctly contextualise OSC documents.</p><h4>Dependencies flags</h4><p>In future iterations, structured documents could be structured so as to support studies of the emergence and spread of regulatory good practices, as well as comparative studies of regulation. This can be achieved by cross-referencing external content that the document is meant to harmonise with or implement, at document or sub-document level; and by separately flagging any deliberate deviations from national/international baselines.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543446" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543446" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543446">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Accessibility and delivery  
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543446" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543446">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Preferred structured document formats</h4><p>Structured documents should be in JSON or XML format for ease of delivery and implementation, avoiding PDF or HTML as primary formats. PDF and HTML formats will continue to be made available to support human readability.</p><h4>Data accessible via API</h4><p>Content should be made available to authorised users via bulk API queries.</p><h4>Developer onboarding</h4><p>Robust documentation, self-service tools, content catalogue resources and administrator facilities should be provided (as opposed to in-person, high-touch support).</p><h4>Stable APIs</h4><p>Low API downtimes should be ensured; and furthermore, support fast access to newly published documents with explicit service level commitments.</p><h4>Alternative platform distribution</h4><p>GitHub or similar platforms as an alternative to APIs will be considered for ease of access and reuse by certain users, e.g. academics and specially authorised users.</p><h4>Human-readable parity</h4><p>Structured documents should match with cached copies for firms without RegTech capabilities, or firms and vendors that need to superimpose metadata over the original text.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543451" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543451" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543451">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Distribution framework 
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543451" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543451">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Template content license</h4><p>Template licensing agreements and terms of use will be considered, likely with provisions for different categories of users, providing separately for commercial or academic use and re-distribution.</p><h4>Funding and access model</h4><p>A framework for content costing should be prepared, perhaps allowing for free access to certain user categories.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543456" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543456" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543456">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Publisher-side compliance and third-party risk  
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543456" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543456">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Structured redaction</h4><p>Though personal data are not likely to occur in the basic regulatory framework, annotation tools should provide the ability to flag or restrict personal data or confidential content, so that only sanitised content is delivered to the public. Structured documents should contain metadata reflecting such restrictions and flag content for redaction before distribution.</p><h4>Third-party attribution and support for alternative terms of use</h4><p>Annotation tools should allow documents to be allocated to collections according to their author and terms of use to distinguish between content owned by third parties and content that is subject to the OSC’s own terms of use. Structured documents should contain flags for in-text third party content so that it can be safely and easily redacted.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543461" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543461" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543461">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Implementation Strategy and User Relations 
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543461" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543461">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <h4>Narrow data launch and iteration</h4><p>A pilot of the machine-readable data set with a focused section of rules / instruments will be considered, which can be further refined and expanded based on user feedback.</p><h4>User communications plan</h4><p>A published implementation and content roadmap should be considered, as well as committing to advance notice of ontology changes.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


      



<div class="accordion-card">
  <a class="accordion-card__header collapsed" id="accordionHeading543466" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#accordionContent543466" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="accordionContent543466">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Title</div>

      Alignment with widely adopted machine-readable formats  
  
  </a>
  <div id="accordionContent543466" class="collapse accordion-card__content print-visible rich-text" aria-labelledby="accordionHeading543466">
      <div class="label field__label visually-hidden">Content</div>

      <p>In devising a document metadata framework, there is a case for aligning with publication schemata such as the Oasis Consortium’s Akoma Ntoso, an LegalXML markup standard or the European Union’s European Legislation Identifier (ELI) standard.</p><p>The framework proposed in <a href="https://www.osc.ca/en/securities-law/instruments-rules-policies/transforming-how-we-share-ontarios-securities-regulation#!tabContent543486" target="_blank">Tab 4: Example framework</a> draws on key elements and principles of these standards. In particular, the proposed framework:</p><ul><li data-list-item-id="e06028a55df4a582f0739e88d2c72b215">is document oriented and technology neutral</li><li data-list-item-id="eabd37afcff3f2a32cd3a240fa41fd0b5">envisages each document as consisting of a series of blocks as Akoma Ntoso and ELI do, so that the actionable elements of any regulatory text can be isolated from the merely bibliographical and from tables of contents.</li><li data-list-item-id="e0dad1cc98423d3bd53aef04a2f5693d1">accounts for some of the document-to-document relationships accounted for in AKN and ELI (repeal/repeals; substitution/amends; insertion/changes per <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/fullHtml/10.1145/3614321.3614327">Loutsaris et al (2023)</a>)</li><li data-list-item-id="e76ff92ee17f05f655b638574efb1ce50">physically separates the ‘authorial’ layer of text applied by the regulatory author in order to set out the rules/law and their rationale, from the ‘editorial’ layer of text applied by the author or the editor in order to explain why the text is the way it is or where certain references come from.</li><li data-list-item-id="e2cad7d7aa4927623200c24f2808a81f1">acknowledges, in metadata, that each document is part of a regulatory process – in this case by allowing for different document statuses and even the individual publishing task it originates from.</li></ul><p>Having said this a case for full alignment has not emerged from discussions and the proposed example framework does not assume this goal.</p>
  
  </div>
</div>


  
        </div>
            <div class="swiper-pagination"></div>
    </div>
    
  </div>

</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>


      

<div class="tab-item tab-item--default tab-pane fade" id="tabContent543486" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="nav-tab-543486">
      <h2 class="print-only">
      Example framework  
  </h2>
        <div class="tab-item__content">
      
      




<section id="section543581"  class="text-section text-section--default with-bullet container section section--default has-anchor section543581">
    
  
    <div class="title">
    <h2>Example framework  </h2>
  </div>
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <blockquote style="border:2px solid #002d42;margin-bottom:15px;padding:20px;"><p>The OSC is exploring the idea of publishing a machine-readable dataset of our regulatory instruments to facilitate access to our regulatory framework and reduce burden for capital markets participants. We are seeking input from stakeholders on our initial explorations and next steps. We encourage you to review the sample metadata schema, provide feedback on licensing preferences and use cases, identify priority content for conversion to a machine-readable format, and participate in future consultations on taxonomy governance and implementation strategy.</p></blockquote><h4>What a machine-readable regulatory framework could look like</h4><p>This article details a suggested framework for that machine readable dataset.</p><p>This is a proposed metadata schema to help visualize the suggested format and solicit targeted feedback from stakeholders on its usability and completeness. Stakeholders are invited to consider these and propose adjustments to the OSC.</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



      






<section id="section543506"  class="card-collection card-collection--default card-collection--collapsible-content card-collection-layout-1-col transparent section section--default has-anchor section543506">
      <div class="container">

    
<div class="section-header">
    
    </div>


    <div class="card-collection__content_wrapper">
      
                  <div class="collapsible-search-wrapper">
            <div class="collapsible-content-search-wrapper"><div class="js-form-item form-item js-form-type-select form-type-select js-form-item- form-item-">
      <label>Quicklinks</label>
        <select class="collapsible-content-search form-select"><option value="">- Select -</option><option value="blocks-schema">Document Blocks Schema  </option><option value="document-schema">Document Schema  </option><option value="publisher-schema">Publisher Schema</option><option value="sub-document-schema">Sub-document Schema  </option><option value="semantic-structure-schema">Semantic structure schema</option><option value="publishing-workflow-schema">Publishing workflow schema</option></select>
        </div>
</div>
            <div>
              <button class="collapsible-search--expand-all button cta-outlined form-submit">Expand All</button>
              <button class="collapsible-search--close-all button cta-outlined form-submit">Close All</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        
        <div class="collapsible" id="collapsibleGroup543506">
          
      


<a tabindex="-1" id="blocks-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingblocks-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentblocks-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentblocks-schema">
    
      <div>
      Document Blocks Schema  
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentblocks-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingblocks-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543491"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543491">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>Title page</h5><p>The title page of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Bibliographical notes</h5><p>The bibliographical notes of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Table of contents</h5><p>The table of contents of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Introduction</h5><p>The introductory part of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Justification</h5><p>The justification of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Main content</h5><p>The main body of the document's content. Can contain signposts.</p><h5>Conclusions</h5><p>The concluding sections of the document. Cannot contain signposts.</p><h5>Attachments</h5><p>Must only contain an array of document_id attributes.</p><h5>Third Party Content</h5><p>Any block-level third party content which needs to be separated from the rest of the document. Cannot contain signposts</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

      


<a tabindex="-1" id="document-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingdocument-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentdocument-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentdocument-schema">
    
      <div>
      Document Schema  
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentdocument-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingdocument-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543511"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543511">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>document_id</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; (Document ID)</p><p>The unique identifier for this regulatory document, generated as a hash of the document content. Helps uniquely address documents in the pipeline. Note any degree of deviation will result in a new unique ID even if the text is unchanged.</p><h5>processed</h5><p>string &lt;ISO 8601&gt; (Processed)</p><p>The date and time when the document was last taken through the digital content pipeline. Helps recall faulty documents by unambiguously identifying the erroneous release they were part of.</p><h5>status</h5><p>string (Status)</p><p>Indicates what the legal status of the document is. Helps users identify and remove invalidated documents.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'In force', 'Repealed', 'Repealed and replaced'</p><h5>date_invalidated</h5><p>string &lt;ISO 8601&gt; or null (Date Invalidated)</p><p>For invalid documents only: The date and time when the document was invalidated. Helps users identify potential noncompliance due to out-of-date documentation.</p><h5>draft</h5><p>string (Draft)</p><p>Indicates if the document is a draft with the intention to republish in a more final form in future. Helps distinguish between documents that are in effect and those not yet finalized.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'Y' / 'N'</p><h5>upstream_draft</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Upstream Draft)</p><p>For documents that have previously been published in a draft form only. The document ID of the document's draft version, if applicable. Must be a valid document ID.</p><h5>amended</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Amended)</p><p>The document ID of a document amended by the present document.</p><h5>replacement</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Replacement)</p><p>The document ID of the document that replaces the present document.</p><h5>replaces</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Replaces)</p><p>The document ID of a document replaced by the present document.</p><h5>repeals</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Repeals)</p><p>The document ID of a document repealed by the present document.</p><h5>repealed by</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Repealed by)</p><p>The document ID of a document that repeals the present document.</p><h5>published</h5><p>string &lt;Date&gt; or null (Published)</p><p>The date when the document was officially published. Helps users identify the correct version of a document.</p><h5>publishers</h5><p>Array of strings or null (Publishers)</p><p>An array of publishers for the document. Helps distinguish document ownership.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary could include: 'OSC', 'CSA', 'Parliament of Canada', 'Ontario Legislature', 'Ontario Government', 'CIRO', 'OFSI', 'FINTRAC', 'Government of Canada', 'Information and Privacy Commission of Ontario', 'Third Party'.</p><h5>versionable</h5><p>string (Versionable)</p><p>A flag indicating if the document is subject to versioning. Helps identify document version clusters.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'Y' / 'N'</p><h5>thirdparty</h5><p>string (Third Party)</p><p>A flag for entire documents that the OSC cannot license.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'Y' / 'N'</p><h5>title</h5><p>string (Title)</p><p>The intended, citable title for the document. Helps users search for a precise document.</p><h5>collection</h5><p>string (Collection)</p><p>The OSC document collection the document belongs to. Helps users search and allows for content curation. Must be one of the OSC's list of pre-defined collections.</p><h5>nns_d1</h5><p>integer or null (NNS Digit 1)</p><p>The first digit of the National Numbering System (NNS) code, indicating the broad subject area. See nns_d1_narrative for descriptions.</p><h5>nns_d1_narrative</h5><p>string or null (NNS D1 Narrative)</p><p>The narrative description for the first digit of the NNS code.</p><ul><li data-list-item-id="e13d811d7af0a49c64a11ed95a527722e">1: Procedures and Related Matters</li><li data-list-item-id="e8493f4c9b2e2b0ef72e3f314f6d9ce4c">2: Certain Capital Market Participants</li><li data-list-item-id="ec1f21ce82ac297268ddbb8d64e899a40">3: Registration Requirements and Related Matters</li><li data-list-item-id="e997d5888abb11e1fdf8f4b77d9e85526">4: Distribution Requirements</li><li data-list-item-id="e515489ba252a5c7b3a1655c1eb24a6fb">5: Ongoing Requirements for Issuers and Insiders</li><li data-list-item-id="e73a448839b9bd48046305bfa19291a67">6: Take-over Bids and Special Transactions</li><li data-list-item-id="e52db2a8916b10829a9bc0f0437a93df8">7: Securities Transactions Outside the Jurisdiction</li><li data-list-item-id="e5c5cd68517a3bff7d2e46a3f017cf296">8: Investment Funds</li><li data-list-item-id="e179a17db93b4a17414e1751ef59933cd">9: Derivatives</li></ul><h5>nns_d2</h5><p>integer or null (NNS Digit 2)</p><p>The second digit of the NNS code, indicating the narrow subject area. See nns_d2_narrative for descriptions.</p><h5>nns_d2_narrative</h5><p>string or null (NNS D2 Narrative)</p><p>The narrative description for the second digit of the NNS code.</p><table class="table table-hover" style="width:100%;" border="1" cellspacing="4" cellpadding="4"><tbody><tr style="background-color:#c6d9f1;"><td><strong>Category</strong></td><td><strong>Code(s) and description</strong></td></tr><tr><td>1 - Procedure and Related Matters</td><td>1 - General<br>2 - Applications<br>3 - Filings with Securities Regulatory Authority<br>4 - Definitions<br>5 - Hearings and Enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>2 - Certain Capital Market Participants</td><td>1 - Stock Exchanges<br>2 - Other Markets<br>3 - Trading Rules<br>4 - Clearing and Settlement<br>5 - Other Participants</td></tr><tr><td>3 - Registration and Related Matters and Related Matters&nbsp;</td><td>1 - Registration Requirements&nbsp;<br>2 - Registration Exemptions&nbsp;<br>3 - Ongoing Requirements Affecting Registrants&nbsp;<br>4 - Fitness for Registration&nbsp;<br>5 - Non-Resident Registrants&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>4 - Distribution Requirements&nbsp;</td><td>1 - Prospectus Contents - Non-Financial Matters&nbsp;<br>2 - Prospectus Contents - Financial Matters&nbsp;<br>3 - Prospectus Filing Matters&nbsp;<br>4 - Alternative Forms of Prospectus&nbsp;<br>5 - Prospectus Exempt Distributions&nbsp;<br>6 - Requirements Affecting Distributions by Certain Issuers&nbsp;<br>7 - Advertising and Marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>8 - Distribution Restrictions&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;">5 - Ongoing Requirements for Issuers and Insiders&nbsp;</td><td>1 - Disclosure – General&nbsp;<br>2 - Financial Disclosure&nbsp;<br>3 - Timely Disclosure&nbsp;<br>4 - Proxy Solicitation&nbsp;<br>5 - Insider Reporting&nbsp;<br>6 - Restricted Shares&nbsp;<br>7 - Cease Trading Orders&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>8 - Corporate Governance&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;">6 - Take-over Bids and Special Transactions</td><td>1 - Special Transactions&nbsp;<br>2 - Take-over Bids&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;">7 - Securities Transactions Outside the Jurisdictions&nbsp;</td><td>1 - International Issuers&nbsp;<br>2 - Distributions Outside the Jurisdiction&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;">8 - Investment Funds&nbsp;</td><td>1 - Investment Fund Distributions&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;">9 - Derivatives&nbsp;</td><td>1 - General&nbsp;<br>2 - Trading<br>3 - Registration and Regulation of OTC Derivatives Market Participants<br>4 - Clearing and Cleared Derivatives<br>5 - Uncleared Derivatives<br>6 - Data Reporting</td></tr></tbody></table><h5>&nbsp;</h5><h5>nns_d3</h5><p>integer or null (NNS Digit 3)</p><p>The third digit of the NNS code, indicating the document type. See nns_d3_narrative for descriptions.</p><h5>nns_d3_narrative</h5><p>string or null (NNS D3 Narrative)</p><p>The narrative description for the third digit of the NNS code.</p><ul><li data-list-item-id="ec2d93f38d2821f821ed5ae5a3e571592">1: National Instrument/Multilateral Instrument and any related Companion Policy or Form(s)</li><li data-list-item-id="ee64d7fb4642f63072b9d28762e1c9db4">2: National Policy/Multilateral Policy</li><li data-list-item-id="ebc694c11a8b6ec4292c4bb2308452edd">3: CSA Notice</li><li data-list-item-id="e9ade53175c8464a2aca597f3297ecd5c">4: CSA Concept Proposal or Discussion Paper</li><li data-list-item-id="e5086213885a27d2f283e7ab5cf715990">5: Local Rule, Regulation or Blanket Order or Ruling</li><li data-list-item-id="e91b140a1223cdd4e88d3ec94a82b901c">6: Local Policy</li><li data-list-item-id="e910943ef28b59ee1eda53f65204aa5f9">7: Local Notice</li><li data-list-item-id="e4d999f66ceac9f55174992da9161f53c">8: Implementing Instrument</li><li data-list-item-id="efa6e4b6cf9939000deefab6f4c0d39e1">9: Miscellaneous</li></ul><h5>domain_d45</h5><p>integer or null (Domain Sort Number)</p><p>The sorting number of the document within its sub-domain (a serial number determined chronologically).</p><h5>parent_doc_l1</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Parent Document L1)</p><p>The document ID of the National Law referenced by the present document. Helps trace regulatory lineage.</p><h5>parent_doc_l2</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Parent Document L2)</p><p>The document ID of the National/Multilateral Instrument referenced by the present document. Helps trace regulatory lineage.</p><h5>parent_doc_l3</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Parent Document L3)</p><p>The document ID of the Provincial Law referenced by the present document. Helps trace regulatory lineage.</p><h5>consultation_doc</h5><p>string &lt;uuid&gt; or null (Consultation Document)</p><p>The document ID of the OSC document in which the OSC consults on the proposed text of the present document. Helps trace regulatory lineage.</p><h5>effective_doc</h5><p>string &lt;Date&gt; or null (Effective Document Date)</p><p>The date when the first obligation in the present document came, or will come into, effect. Helps users plan for compliance.</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

      


<a tabindex="-1" id="publisher-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingpublisher-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentpublisher-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentpublisher-schema">
    
      <div>
      Publisher Schema
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentpublisher-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingpublisher-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543531"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543531">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>jurisdiction</h5><p>string (Jurisdiction)</p><p>Jurisdiction to which a document applies. Helps users identify the origin of regulations.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'Canada', 'Ontario', 'International', 'Other'</p><h5>license_collection</h5><p>string (Licence Collection)</p><p>Denotes membership of a collection of documents sharing the same licencing terms. The list of collections is defined and extensible by the OSC.</p><h5>publishing_id</h5><p>string (Publishing ID)</p><p>Denotes the identity of the publishing team behind the document.</p><h5>license_url</h5><p>string &lt;URL&gt; (License URL)</p><p>The URL to the full license document and terms of use. The URL is determined by the license_collection.</p><h5>license_notice</h5><p>string (License Notice)</p><p>Standard copyright notice provided on behalf of the publisher. The notice is determined by the ‘publishers’ attribute.</p><h5>license_terms</h5><p>string (License Terms)</p><p>The publisher's full Terms of Use in a machine-readable string. The terms are determined by the license_collection.</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

      


<a tabindex="-1" id="sub-document-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingsub-document-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentsub-document-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentsub-document-schema">
    
      <div>
      Sub-document Schema  
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentsub-document-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingsub-document-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543541"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543541">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>signpost_id</h5><p>integer (Signpost ID)</p><p>The unique integer ID of the signpost.</p><h5>implements</h5><p>Array or null (Implement Signpost 1)</p><p>The document and sub-document ID of the first rule or provision that the present signpost is intended to implement or clarify. Must be a valid document and sub-document ID.</p><h5>aligns</h5><p>Array or null (Aligns)</p><p>The document and optionally sub-document ID of the rules or provisions that the present signpost is intended to align to. Must be a valid document and optionally sub-document ID.</p><h5>derogations</h5><p>Array or null (Derogates)</p><p>The document and optionally sub-document ID of the rules or provisions that the present signpost is deliberately not aligning to. Must be a valid document and optionally sub-document ID.</p><h5>effective_signpost</h5><p>string &lt;Date&gt; or null (Effective Signpost Date)</p><p>The date when the obligation in the present signpost came, or will come into, effect. Helps users plan for compliance.</p><h5>is_personal_data</h5><p>Boolean (Is Personal Data)</p><p>Identifies whether the block contains personally identifying information to enable redaction.</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

      


<a tabindex="-1" id="semantic-structure-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingsemantic-structure-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentsemantic-structure-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentsemantic-structure-schema">
    
      <div>
      Semantic structure schema
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentsemantic-structure-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingsemantic-structure-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543551"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543551">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>node_code</h5><p>string (Node Code)</p><p>The human-readable identifier for the node, as derived from the text.</p><h5>node_label</h5><p>string or null (Node Label)</p><p>The human-readable long-form description or title for the node, as derived from the text.</p><h5>node_text</h5><p>string (Node Text)</p><p>The actual text or transcript of the relevant node in the regulatory document's structure.</p><h5>node_snippet_id</h5><p>string (Node Snippet ID)</p><p>A unique identifier based on the node's physical position or structure within the source document, delimited in '.' format.</p><h5>node_semantic_id</h5><p>string or null (Node Semantic ID)</p><p>Captures the logical or semantic position of the node within the document, delimited in '/' format.</p><h5>node_signpost_ids</h5><p>array [integer] or null (Node Signpost ID)</p><p>Captures the signpost ids that are linked to that node.</p><h5>is_definition</h5><p>boolean (Is Definition)</p><p>Captures whether the given node and its children are part of a definition.</p><h5>reference_file</h5><p>string or null (Reference File)</p><p>Details the file name containing the attached table or image from the document.</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

      


<a tabindex="-1" id="publishing-workflow-schema"></a>
<div class="collapsible-card">
  <a class="collapsible-card__header collapsed" id="collapsibleHeadingpublishing-workflow-schema" role="button" data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapsibleContentpublishing-workflow-schema" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsibleContentpublishing-workflow-schema">
    
      <div>
      Publishing workflow schema
    </div>
  
  </a>
  <div id="collapsibleContentpublishing-workflow-schema" class="collapse collapsible-card__content print-visible" aria-labelledby="collapsibleHeadingpublishing-workflow-schema">
    <div class="collapsible-card__content__inner">
      
      




<section id="section543561"  class="text-section text-section--default container section section--default has-anchor section543561">
    
  
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <h5>ticket_id</h5><p>string (Ticket ID)</p><p>The ID of the ticket related to the processing of the present document.</p><h5>ticket_status</h5><p>string (Ticket Status)</p><p>Indicates what the status of the ticket is.</p><p>Controlled Vocabulary: 'Backlog - Out for decision', 'Backlog - Decision received', 'Drafting in progress', 'Management reviewed / for Sign-off', 'Board Approved for Publication'</p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

  
        </div>
            <div class="swiper-pagination"></div>
    </div>
    
  </div>

</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>


      

<div class="tab-item tab-item--default tab-pane fade" id="tabContent543576" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="nav-tab-543576">
      <h2 class="print-only">
      Provide feedback
  </h2>
        <div class="tab-item__content">
      
      




<section id="section543571"  class="text-section text-section--default with-bullet container section section--default has-anchor section543571">
    
  
    <div class="title">
    <h2>Provide feedback</h2>
  </div>
    
    <div class="content rich-text">
      <p>These articles are based on a collaboration with <a href="https://reg-genome.com/">RegGenome</a>, a regulatory data technology company. Their support informed a review of the literature on digital legal and regulatory publishing, three technical workshops with vendors, industry and professional firm representatives, a senior-level industry roundtable, and targeted interviews with academics and regulatory technologists. We are also grateful to the stakeholders who have already contributed to our work to date.</p><p>We want to hear your feedback on machine-readable rules, including from stakeholders who will directly benefit from this new publication format.</p><p>Consider the following questions:</p><ul><li data-list-item-id="ec3c0cf05669d668e8d21c1f62d605262">Where do you see the greatest potential impact of this initiative? What internal capacity, governance, or risk considerations could limit that impact? Are there any unintended consequences we should anticipate?</li><li data-list-item-id="e0b23be9583d445e427768c116e7a55e5">In addition to prioritizing legally binding regulatory material and rules most frequently referred to, are there additional factors we should consider when determining our approach in prioritizing the order of converting documents?</li><li data-list-item-id="e359728b4eb59acea350c2cfbf0cef1c5">Do the presented use cases accurately and comprehensively reflect the ways in which market participants could realistically use this dataset? If not, what additional realistic use cases should be considered?</li><li data-list-item-id="edc3fa38d3d6c083349579515ccd534de">Do the proposed business requirements capture what market participants need to effectively access and use this dataset? What additional business requirements could be considered?</li><li data-list-item-id="e8984e15dee545e49fd397da58f293c18">Are there specific licensing and/or distribution provisions for this dataset that would better support the needs of market participants?</li><li data-list-item-id="ef33145db7a2c87dc2dce8b789d777a07">Are there elements in the draft sample framework that could be clarified or expanded to support practical use?</li><li data-list-item-id="e9580c45a037edbbd7b81f07e3c8aaf59">Based on your experience, do you see any other quantifiable benefits or anticipated savings (e.g. time, cost)?</li></ul><p>While our feedback period has ended, we continue to accept comments on an ongoing basis at <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#24564143514845504b565d5056454a57424b564945504d4b4a644b57470a4745"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="0a786f6d7f666b7e6578737e786b64796c6578676b7e6365644a65796924696b">[email&#160;protected]</span></a>.</p><p>Please refer your questions to:</p><p>Levin Karg&nbsp;<br>Head of Modernizing Regulation&nbsp;<br>Regulatory Transformation and Strategic Research<br><a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#d9b5b2b8abbe99b6aabaf7beb6aff7b6b7f7bab8"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="b4d8dfd5c6d3f4dbc7d79ad7d5">[email&#160;protected]</span></a></p><p>Christina Furlano&nbsp;<br>Senior Knowledge Analyst&nbsp;<br>Regulatory Transformation and Strategic Research&nbsp;<br><a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#c1a2a7b4b3ada0afae81aeb2a2efa2a0"><span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="543732212638353a3b143b27377a3735">[email&#160;protected]</span></a></p>
  </div>
  
  
</section>



  
    </div>
  </div>


  
  </div>

  </div>
</div>


  
</article>

